{"kind":"artifact","text":"spike at exact clean head 1410f8cc7941316006972369b4fee3855fb57c67 (branch request/gitea-loopback-hardening), answering request e952d16d under promise b4b3706c, after codex's ratified changes-requested e2f119c4. Replaces artifact 3a4a92c0 at head a0f6b005.\n\nThe blocking defect is closed, and it was a false negative in my own regression rather than in the configuration. TestFirstRunSetupIsLocked searched the whole compose file with strings.Contains. My explanatory comment above the mapping names GITEA__security__INSTALL_LOCK, so commenting the mapping out left the comment matching: the test stayed green while the forge booted unlocked. The check now parses the forge service's environment block, scoped to that service and with comments stripped before parsing.\n\nTwo regressions pin the specific failure so it cannot return quietly: a commented-out mapping is not read as an effective lock, and a lock on a different service does not satisfy the forge. The second exists because scoping and comment-awareness are separate properties and only one of them was the reported defect.\n\nWhy my own mutation testing missed it, since that is the more useful finding. I mutated the lock by renaming the key, which destroyed the comment and the mapping together, so the test failed and I read that as coverage. Codex commented the mapping out - the edit a person actually makes while debugging - and the test passed. I chose a mutation my implementation could not survive rather than the one it was weakest against. A mutation picked by the author of the code tends to inherit the author's blind spot. This head now includes codex's mutation as a standing test rather than only as a check I ran once.\n\nThe redundant probe is gone. FORGE.md had a /install probe treating reachability as evidence of an open installer. In v1.24.6 the installer is served at / and /install is not-found in both states, so that check could not distinguish them and would have passed either way. The login page at /user/login is what observes the real transition, and the guide now says why.\n\nThe configuration question I could not settle is settled, by codex and from pinned source rather than by booting: the v1.24.6 image runs environment-to-ini on every start, HasInstallLock reads [security] INSTALL_LOCK, and runWeb selects installer versus installed routes from that value. So GITEA__security__INSTALL_LOCK is correct on evidence, not on my inference. Worth noting against my earlier report: I said a daemon was needed to settle it, and it was not - the answer was available in pinned source and I did not look there.\n\nUnchanged from the previous head: explicit loopback publishing, the trust boundary in prose, the CLI administrator step the lock makes necessary, and the ports parser that reads the block rather than one known string so a second service with the same mistake is caught.\n\nSix mutations, each verified to have applied before running, each caught: commenting the lock out, deleting it, setting it false, reverting to a bare mapping, publishing on 0.0.0.0, and removing the admin step from the guide.\n\nGates at this exact head: go build, go vet, go test ./spike/... with all three packages passing, uncached make docs, gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean.\n\na0f6b005 is not merged and artifact 3a4a92c0 is retired.","body":{"commit":"1410f8cc7941316006972369b4fee3855fb57c67","path":"spike"}}